.TH VIC 1 "2026-09-06" "vic v1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vic \- a small vi/vim-compatible text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vic
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIfile\fR [\fIfile\fR \&...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vic
is a small, dependency-free vi clone.
It supports multiple modes (Normal, Insert, Replace, Visual, Command),
a full motion and operator set, named registers, marks, undo/redo,
syntax highlighting, and a built-in
.B :run
transformation engine.
.PP
Multiple files may be given on the command line.
.B :n
advances to the next file;
.B :prev
(or
.BR :p )
goes back;
.B :rew
restarts from the first.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-R
Open file(s) in read-only mode.
.TP
.BI \-c " cmd"
Execute the ex command
.I cmd
after opening the first file.
.TP
.B \-H
Print the feature list and exit.
.TP
.B \-h
Print usage and exit.
.SH MODES
.B vic
operates in seven modes.
.TS
l l l.
Mode	Enter	Leave
_
Normal	startup / \fBESC\fR	\(em
Insert	\fBi a I A o O s S\fR	\fBESC\fR
Replace	\fBR\fR	\fBESC\fR
Visual char	\fBv\fR	\fBESC\fR or \fBv\fR
Visual line	\fBV\fR	\fBESC\fR or \fBV\fR
Visual block	\fBCtrl-V\fR	\fBESC\fR or \fBCtrl-V\fR
Command	\fB:\fR	\fBEnter\fR / \fBESC\fR
.TE
.SH NORMAL MODE
.SS Counts
Any motion or operator may be prefixed with a count.
Examples:
.B 3j
moves down 3 lines;
.B d2w
deletes 2 words;
.B 5>>
indents 5 lines.
.SS Cursor Motion
.TS
l l.
\fBh\fR / \fBl\fR	left / right
\fBj\fR / \fBk\fR	down / up (column preserved across short lines)
Arrow keys	same as \fBh j k l\fR
\fBEnter\fR	next line, first non-blank
\fB\-\fR	previous line, first non-blank
\fBw\fR / \fBW\fR	forward to next word / WORD start
\fBb\fR / \fBB\fR	backward to word / WORD start
\fBe\fR / \fBE\fR	forward to word / WORD end
\fB0\fR	beginning of line
\fB^\fR	first non-blank of line
\fB$\fR	end of line
\fBgg\fR / \fB1G\fR	first line
\fBG\fR	last line (or line N with \fBNG\fR)
\fBH M L\fR	top / middle / bottom of screen
\fB{ }\fR	previous / next paragraph
\fBCtrl-F\fR / \fBCtrl-B\fR	page forward / backward
\fBCtrl-D\fR / \fBCtrl-U\fR	half-page down / up
\fBCtrl-E\fR / \fBCtrl-Y\fR	scroll one line down / up
\fB%\fR	jump to matching bracket \fB()[]{}\fR
\fBf\fR / \fBF\fR	find char forward / backward on line
\fBt\fR / \fBT\fR	to just before char forward / backward
\fB;\fR / \fB,\fR	repeat last f/F/t/T forward / backward
.TE
.SS Search
.TS
l l.
\fB/\fIpat\fR	search forward for \fIpat\fR (POSIX ERE)
\fB?\fIpat\fR	search backward
\fBn\fR	repeat search in same direction
\fBN\fR	repeat search in opposite direction
\fB*\fR	search forward for word under cursor
\fB#\fR	search backward for word under cursor
\fBg*\fR	like \fB*\fR but without whole-word boundary
\fBg#\fR	like \fB#\fR but without whole-word boundary
.TE
.SS Marks
.TS
l l.
\fBma\fR	set mark \fIa\fR at current position
\fB'a\fR	jump to line containing mark \fIa\fR
\fB''\fR	jump to previous context position
.TE
Marks
.B a\(endz
are available.
Visual mode automatically sets
.B '<
and
.BR '> .
.SS Operators
Operators act on a motion:
.BR d$ ,
.BR c2w ,
.BR y'a ,
etc.
Doubling an operator applies it to the current line:
.BR dd ,
.BR cc ,
.BR yy ,
.BR >> ,
.BR << .
.TS
l l.
\fBd\fR	delete
\fBc\fR	change (delete + insert)
\fBy\fR	yank (copy)
\fB>\fR	indent right
\fB<\fR	indent left
\fB~\fR	toggle case
.TE
.SS Operator Shortcuts
.TS
l l.
\fBD\fR	delete to end of line (\fBd$\fR)
\fBC\fR	change to end of line (\fBc$\fR)
\fBx\fR	delete character under cursor
\fBX\fR	delete character before cursor
\fBs\fR	substitute character (= \fBcl\fR)
\fBS\fR	substitute line (= \fBcc\fR)
\fBJ\fR	join current line with next
\fBr\fR	replace single character
.TE
.SS Text Objects
Text objects are used inside operators.
.TS
l l.
\fBiw\fR	inner word
\fBaw\fR	a word (includes surrounding whitespace)
\fBi(\fR	inner () block
\fBa(\fR	outer () block
\fBi[\fR	inner [] block
\fBi{\fR	inner {} block
\fBit\fR	inner XML/HTML tag
\fBi"\fR	inner double-quoted string
\fBi'\fR	inner single-quoted string
.TE
.SS Put / Paste
.TS
l l.
\fBp\fR	put after cursor / below line
\fBP\fR	put before cursor / above line
.TE
.SS Registers
Prefix any yank, delete, or put with
.BI \&" x
to use named register
.I x
.RB ( a \(en z ).
The
.B "+
register reads and writes the shared (clipboard) register,
backed by
.IR ~/.cache/vic/yank .
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
"ayy        yank current line into register a
"ap         put contents of register a
"+p         put from shared register
.fi
.RE
.SS Undo / Redo
.TS
l l.
\fBu\fR	undo
\fBCtrl-R\fR	redo
\fBU\fR	restore current line to state at entry
.TE
.SS Repeat
.B \&.
repeats the last modifying command (including its count and register).
.SS Miscellaneous
.TS
l l.
\fBCtrl-L\fR	redraw screen
\fBCtrl-G\fR	show status (filename, line count)
\fBZZ\fR	write and quit
\fBZQ\fR	quit without writing
.TE
.SH INSERT MODE
Enter insert mode with
.B i
(before cursor),
.B a
(after cursor),
.B I
(line start),
.B A
(line end),
.B o
(new line below),
.B O
(new line above).
.TS
l l.
\fBESC\fR	return to Normal mode
\fBCtrl-H\fR / \fBBackspace\fR	backspace
\fBCtrl-D\fR	remove one indent level
\fBCtrl-V\fR	insert next character literally
.TE
.PP
With
.B autoindent
set, new lines inherit the indentation of the previous line.
With
.B expandtab
set, Tab inserts spaces instead of a tab character.
.SH REPLACE MODE
.B R
enters Replace mode: typed characters overwrite existing text.
.B r
replaces only the character under the cursor, then returns to Normal mode.
.SH VISUAL MODE
.B v
enters character-wise visual mode;
.B V
enters line-wise visual mode;
.B Ctrl-V
enters block (column) visual mode.
Extend the selection with any motion command.
Pressing
.BR v ,
.BR V ,
or
.B Ctrl-V
while already in visual mode switches sub-mode without leaving visual.
.B o
swaps the cursor and anchor, so you can extend the selection from the other end.
.SS Character and Line Operators
.TS
l l.
\fBd\fR	delete selection
\fBc\fR	change selection
\fBy\fR	yank selection
\fB>\fR	indent selection
\fB<\fR	de-indent selection
\fB~\fR	toggle case of selection
\fB:\fR	enter command line with \fB'<,'>\fR pre-filled
.TE
.SS Block Visual Mode (Ctrl-V)
The anchor and cursor define a column rectangle.
All block operators skip rows that do not reach the left column of the block.
.TS
l l.
\fBd\fR	delete the column rectangle
\fBy\fR	yank the column rectangle
\fBc\fR	delete rectangle and enter insert mode
\fBI\fR	insert text at the left column of every row in the block
\fB>\fR	indent lines covered by the block
\fB<\fR	de-indent lines covered by the block
\fBU\fR	uppercase the rectangle
\fBu\fR	lowercase the rectangle
.TE
After leaving visual mode,
.B '<
and
.B '>
marks point to the start and end of the last selection.
.SH COLON COMMANDS
.SS Address Syntax
Most colon commands accept an address or range before the command name.
The following is an exhaustive list of address forms.
.TS
l l.
\fIN\fR	line number N
\fB.\fR	current line
\fB$\fR	last line
\fB%\fR	whole file (same as \fB1,$\fR)
\fB'a\fR	line containing mark \fIa\fR
\fB'<\fR \fB'>\fR	start/end of last visual selection
\fB/\fIpattern\fB/\fR	next line matching pattern
\fB?\fIpattern\fB?\fR	previous line matching pattern
\fIaddr\fB+\fIN\fR	N lines after addr
\fIaddr\fB-\fIN\fR	N lines before addr
\fIa1\fB,\fIa2\fR	range from a1 to a2
.TE
.SS File and Session Commands
.TS
l l.
\fB:w\fR	write file
\fB:w \fIfilename\fR	write to filename
\fB:w!\fR	force write (overwrite existing)
\fB:q\fR	quit (fails if unsaved changes)
\fB:q!\fR	quit without saving
\fB:wq\fR / \fB:x\fR	write and quit
\fB:wn\fR	write and advance to next file
\fB:e \fIfile\fR	edit file (fails if unsaved; \fB:e!\fR forces)
\fB:n\fR	next file in argument list
\fB:prev\fR / \fB:p\fR	previous file
\fB:rew\fR	rewind to first file
.TE
.SS Editing Commands
.TS
l l.
\fB:[range]d\fR	delete lines
\fB:[range]y\fR	yank lines
\fB:[range]s/\fIF\fB/\fIR\fB/[g]\fR	substitute; \fBg\fR flag replaces all occurrences on line
\fB:[range]g/\fIpat\fB/\fIcmd\fR	run cmd on lines matching pat
\fB:[range]v/\fIpat\fB/\fIcmd\fR	run cmd on lines NOT matching pat
\fB:r \fIfile\fR	read file below cursor
\fB:r! \fIcmd\fR	read shell command output below cursor
\fB:[\fIaddr\fB]\fR	jump to line number
\fB:=\fR	print current line number
\fB:list\fR	list current line showing control characters
.TE
.SS Marks and Info Commands
.TS
l l.
\fB:ma \fIx\fR	set mark \fIx\fR (same as \fBmx\fR)
\fB:fi \fIname\fR	rename current file
\fB:f\fR	reprint file info on status bar
\fB:ve\fR	show version
\fB:fe\fR	show feature list
.TE
.SS Shell Commands
.TS
l l.
\fB:! \fIcmd\fR	run shell command (press Enter to return)
\fB:!\fR	launch \fB$SHELL\fR interactively
.TE
.SS Set Options
.nf
:set                     show all current options
:set \fIoption\fR              enable a boolean option
:set no\fIoption\fR            disable a boolean option
:set \fIoption\fB=\fIvalue\fR        set a value option
.fi
.PP
The following is an exhaustive list of options.
.TS
l l l l.
Option	Abbrev	Default	Meaning
_
\fBautoindent\fR	\fBai\fR	off	new lines inherit indentation
\fBexpandtab\fR	\fBet\fR	off	Tab key inserts spaces
\fBflash\fR	\fBfl\fR	off	flash screen instead of beeping
\fBignorecase\fR	\fBic\fR	off	case-insensitive search
\fBshowmatch\fR	\fBsm\fR	off	briefly jump to matching bracket on insert
\fBtabstop=\fIN\fR	\fBts=\fIN\fR	8	visual width of a tab character
\fBnumber\fR	\fBnu\fR	off	show absolute line numbers
\fBrelativenumber\fR	\fBrnu\fR	off	show relative line numbers
\fBcursorshape=\fIN\fR	\fBcshp=\fIN\fR	\(em	cursor shape in normal mode (1=block 2=underline 3=bar)
\fBcursorshapeinsert=\fIN\fR	\fBcshpi=\fIN\fR	\(em	cursor shape in insert mode
\fBundofile\fR	\fBuf\fR	off	persist undo history across sessions
\fBsyntax\fR	\fBsyn\fR	on	syntax highlighting
.TE
.SH RUN COMMANDS
.B :[range] run
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.PP
Applies a built-in transformation to the buffer or an optional address range.
Without a range, the entire buffer is used.
.SS Text Case
.nf
:run upper          uppercase all text in range
:run lower          lowercase all text in range
.fi
.SS Whitespace
.nf
:run trim           strip trailing whitespace from each line
:run deindent       remove one indent level from each line
:run deindent \fIN\fR     remove N indent levels
:run wrap \fIN\fR         hard-wrap lines at column N (N must be >= 4)
.fi
.SS Lines
.nf
:run sort           sort lines alphabetically
:run sort -r        sort in reverse order
:run uniq           remove consecutive duplicate lines
:run number         prepend "1. 2. 3. \&..." to each line
.fi
.SS Alignment
.nf
:run align \fICHAR\fR     align all lines so the first occurrence of CHAR
                    starts at the same column (padded with spaces)
.fi
.SS Encoding
.nf
:run urlencode      percent-encode the range (RFC 3986 unreserved chars kept)
:run urldecode      decode percent-encoded text (+ decoded as space)
:run base64enc      base-64 encode the range (76-char wrapped lines)
:run base64dec      base-64 decode the range
:run jsonesc        escape the range as a JSON string value
:run jsonunesc      unescape a JSON-escaped string back to plain text
.fi
.SS Statistics
.nf
:run wc             count lines, words, bytes in range
:run col            report maximum line width in range
:run freq           show top-5 identifier frequencies in range
.fi
.SS Hashing (FNV-1a)
.nf
:run hash               hash entire range; show decimal + hex on status bar
:run hash mod \fIN\fR         per-identifier hash%N; report total and collisions
:run hash replace       replace every identifier with its raw 64-bit hash
:run hash replace mod \fIN\fR replace every identifier with hash%N
.fi
.SS Identifier Case Conversion
.nf
:run convert c2s    camelCase \(-> snake_case
:run convert s2c    snake_case \(-> camelCase (lowerCamelCase)
:run convert s2uc   snake_case \(-> UpperCamelCase (PascalCase)
.fi
Only identifier tokens
.RB ( [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]* )
are modified; all other text is passed through unchanged.
.SS Highlight
.nf
:run highlight \fIpattern\fR  highlight all occurrences of pattern in the viewport
:run highlight          clear all highlighting
.fi
.I pattern
is a POSIX BRE regex.
Highlighting is visual-only: it does not move the cursor or affect the buffer.
.SS Debugging
.nf
:run echo \fIargs\fR    print arguments to status bar (no range effect)
.fi
.SH SYNTAX HIGHLIGHTING
Highlighting activates automatically based on file extension.
The following is an exhaustive list of supported languages.
.TS
l l.
Language	Extensions
_
C	\fB.c\fR \fB.h\fR
C++	\fB.cc\fR \fB.cpp\fR \fB.cxx\fR \fB.hpp\fR \fB.hh\fR \fB.hxx\fR
Shell	\fB.sh\fR
Markdown	\fB.md\fR
SQL	\fB.sql\fR
Python	\fB.py\fR
.TE
.PP
Highlighted elements include (non-exhaustive): keywords, numbers, strings,
comments, preprocessor directives (C/C++), and decorators (Python).
.SH STARTUP CONFIGURATION
On startup,
.B vic
reads configuration in the following order (exhaustive list):
.IP 1.
The
.B EXINIT
environment variable, treated as a sequence of ex commands.
.IP 2.
.IR ~/.exrc ,
if it exists, is owned by the current user, and is not group- or world-writable.
.PP
Any valid colon command may appear in these files, one per line.
Example:
.RS
.nf
set ai et ts=4
set ic
set nu
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EXINIT
Sequence of ex commands executed at startup.
.TP
.B SHELL
Used by
.B :!
when launching an interactive shell.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.exrc
User startup configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/vic/yank
Shared register backing the
.B "+
register.
.TP
.I src/config.def.h
Compile-time defaults. Copy to
.I src/config.h
and rebuild to customise.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vi (1),
.BR vim (1),
.BR ex (1)
